        Item 1 (owner round 8 item 1, CLAUDE.md section 6.5 PATH B, chosen by the owner): DataverseUpdateRecordHandler re-stamps INLINE, in the same operation. After the caller's own PATCH has succeeded (still user-OBO), it calls CoreAncestorAfterWriteRestamp.AfterWriteAsync. That is a new sealed helper whose single public member is the after-write re-stamp. It holds only the restamper (no IServiceProvider, no client exposed), writes only stamp columns with values derived from the data, and takes no cancellation token. The queued path for this handler is removed: CoreAncestorRestampQueue.EnqueueAfterWriteAsync, the payload's WrittenColumns and the job handler's after-write branch. The queue and the job handler stay for the storage resolver's stale refusals. AC1 is now MET with no exception: every inventory path re-stamps in the same operation, and a test pins the AI update tool re-stamping the event and the to-do under it in the same call (seed U1: 2 red).
        Item 2 (owner round 8 item 2): TaskActionCore writes the standard ADR-024 regarding pair (id, name, url, and type when a sprk_recordtype_ref row exists). It does so through the to-do builder's own pair method, TodoRegardingBuilder.ApplyResolverPairAsync (step 2 of ApplyResolverFieldsAsync, extracted), not a fork. The name comes from the shared RegardingNameFields map (sprk_communication added, verified live) and is capped at the live 1000. The type comes from ICommunicationDataverseService.QueryRecordTypeRefAsync, the lookup every SDK builder uses. New rows only; nothing is backfilled. Tests pin the pair on a task created under a communication (untyped: no record-type row exists live) and that the reconciliation job then finds a later form clear of that row and clears the orphaned copy (F-051-6), plus the typed case under an invoice (seed T1: 5 red; T2: 1; T3: 2).
