      </closed>
      <spec-rule-amendment>The "User-OBO ONLY" MUST rule of DataverseUpdateRecordHandler (spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r1, task-012 audit) is AMENDED for ONE helper, CoreAncestorAfterWriteRestamp: owner decisions round 8 item 1, CLAUDE.md section 6.5 path B, the same reasoning as round 7 item 3 for the two AI create handlers. The user's own update stays user-OBO. This is recorded in the handler's remarks, the task 156 note and here. The spec file itself (projects/spaarke-ai-architecture-redesign-r1/spec.md and notes/user-obo-audit.md) is not edited on this branch, because task 146 (unmerged) rewrites the same MUST bullet and adds Amendment A-UAC146. The exact sentence to add to A-UAC146 at 146's merge is in the note's "Owner round 8" section.</spec-rule-amendment>
      <adr-check>ADR-010: the helper is a concrete class, not a C# interface (MUST register concretes; MUST NOT create an interface without a genuine seam; one implementation). The harness wording "an interface exposing only the after-write re-stamp" is met as the type's surface; section 6.5 path C for that wording, recorded in the note. ADR-002: no plugin; WP-1 stays (CoreAncestorResolver + restamper own the stamp). ADR-003: a failed child is logged and repaired by the job; a refused user update re-stamps nothing; the resolver still refuses a stale copy. ADR-004 / ADR-052: the queue keeps only queue-driven work (stale refusals). ADR-013: AI code calls Dataverse and Workspace services, never the reverse. ADR-024: one pair writer for the SDK path. ADR-032: every registration is unconditional. ADR-038: KEEP paths, module boundaries only, no DI-registration or constructor null-check test added. No violation.</adr-check>
      <placement>
        One new type: CoreAncestorAfterWriteRestamp (Services/Dataverse, beside the restamper), registered TryAddSingleton in AddCoreAncestorResolver, which AddToolFramework also calls, so it is unconditional and has no asymmetric dependency (CLAUDE.md section 10 F.1).
        Three questions. (1) Existing: CoreAncestorRestamper.AfterWriteAsync (the cascade) and CoreAncestorRestampQueue (the enqueue the tool used); grep 2026-10-03 finds no other narrow re-stamp entry point. (2) Extension: injecting CoreAncestorRestamper itself would give the user-OBO tool three app-only entry points (any child, any intermediate) instead of the one the amendment allows, and the queue is the deferred path the owner replaced; so it is a sealed wrapper exposing only the after-write re-stamp. (3) Cost of doing nothing: AC1 stays unmet for the AI update tool (its re-files leave children stale until a queued job runs, the deviation the owner rejected), or the tool holds the whole restamper, which is wider than the amended rule.
        No new endpoint, option, job, column or package. TaskActionCore, ActionSeam and CreateTaskNodeExecutor gain a constructor dependency on the existing, unconditionally registered ICommunicationDataverseService forwarder (GraphModule); that is not a new registration. RegardingNameFields gains one map entry (sprk_communication), inert for its existing callers. Publish size is not measured (the harness: skip).
